Subject: [PATCH 15/15] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 5, patch 15):
 A CEILING IS A WHOLE NUMBER, AND THE RAISES OF A BASELINE CHAIN

Review 80, agy-002's rows 48 and 49, which are about the two readers of a ceiling that this series changes
(its patch 8). Main made the principal case of each again on these functions, and its third folder lists a
sub-case of each that its probes did not make. Made again here on the commit before this patch; each stands
in the patch's test.

Row 48. A new ceiling of five and a half, with none at the anchor, was read as no ceiling and as no step up,
and the gate that uses the table then lets five through. A ceiling that is not a whole number at or above
zero is refused now, unless it stood so in the first state read. The sub-case, that nothing bounds a further
rise of an entry once it is named as raised: read at its two ends it passes, as before; read along the
history, as patch 8 reads it, the second step needs a second record. That part was closed by patch 8, and
the test here holds it. What is still not bounded is said in the test: one step may be of any height.

Row 49. A raise recorded "from false to true" paid for a rise of one: a truth value is a whole number to the
language. It is refused. The sub-case, intervals that do not chain: the raises were added up, and raises
that add up to the rise could name other numbers than the baseline took. Now a raise starts no higher than
the baseline stood, and the baseline stands no higher than the last raise took it; and, read along the
history, each step up is the raises recorded for it, in order, from where the step starts to where it ends.
A raise that no step took is refused. The other sub-case, a record removed after its step was taken, was
refused already, and the test holds it.

On main nothing moves: both ratchets read as they did, and every ceiling of the attribution table is a whole
number. No test of main's is changed.

Not checked by main beyond the two principal cases. Tested against planted errors by the session that wrote
it; the twins are a chain, a chain with a fall between two raises, and one step recorded as two raises.
